# .sous/.env.local.example
#
# Copy this file to `.sous/.env.local` to set machine-specific values:
#
#     cp .sous/.env.local.example .sous/.env.local
#
# `.sous/.env.local` is gitignored. It is the layer for anything that differs
# between machines or must not be committed: absolute paths outside the repo,
# API tokens, per-user tool preferences.
#
# There is a second, COMMITTED layer: `.sous/.env`. Same syntax, but it is for
# shared team defaults, values your teammates all want the same. Never put a
# secret there, since it is tracked in git.
#
# How it is loaded:
#   - `KEY=VALUE` per line. `#` starts a comment. `export KEY=VALUE` also works.
#   - Loaded into process.env before the config resolves any variables.
#   - Precedence, highest first: your shell, then `.env.local`, then `.env`.
#     So `FOO=bar xcv build` overrides `FOO` from either file for that one run,
#     and a key in `.env.local` overrides the same key in `.env`.
#   - The config reaches these values through its top-level `_env` block, which
#     maps a config variable name to an environment variable name:
#
#         _env: { myPath: "MY_PATH" }
#
#     ...making `${myPath}` available throughout the config.
#
# NOT here: the config-location vars `SOUS_CONFIG`, `SOUS_DIR` and `SOUS_CONFD`
# (and their `--sous-config` / `--sous-dir` / `--sous-confd` flags). Those decide
# where sous looks for `.sous/`, so they are read from the REAL shell environment
# only; setting them in this file would have no effect, since this file is not
# found until after discovery has already run.
#
# sous's own config (`.sous/sous.config.js`) needs NOTHING from this file. It
# derives every path from the `${sousDir}` auto-var, so it has no `_env` block
# and `xcv build` works on a fresh clone with no `.env.local` at all. This
# example exists to document the layer, and as a place to put values if you
# later add a target that needs one.
#
# Example (not used by this repo's config):
# MY_PATH=/absolute/path/on/this/machine
